0,Coast Miwok - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coast_Miwok,"1 month ago - The Coast Miwok are an Indigenous people of California that were the second-largest tribe of the Miwok people. Coast Miwok inhabited the general area of present-day Marin County and southern Sonoma County in Northern California, from the Golden Gate north to Duncans Point and eastward to Sonoma ...","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
1,Miwok: Music — San Geronimo Valley Historical Society,https://www.sgvhistoricalsociety.org/miwok-music,The Catherine A. Callaghan collection of Bodega Miwok sound recordings. Linguistic field recordings: linguistic data; stories; ethnographic data; songs. Citation: Sarah Ballard and Catherine A. Callaghan.,"Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
2,Miwok Music Project - Sound Orchard,https://www.soundorchard.org/miwok-music-project.html,"The Miwok Music Project explores the influence of music created by the indigenous people of Northern California through community singing, language instruction, song recording and documentation, and the creation of new music rooted in Miwok culture.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
3,Coast Miwok Oral Tradition,https://oraltradition.org/coast-miwok-oral-tradition/,"In addition to contributing to a basic knowledge of the oral traditions of the Coast Miwok, the article aims to situate these traditions in the broader context of Central California practices, while also commenting on their general ethnopoetic features.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
4,Coastmiwokofmarin,https://www.coastmiwokofmarin.org/our-history.html,"Loosely divided into five major bands inhabiting the general area that includes today’s Marin and southern Sonoma counties, Coast Miwok were the second largest group of Miwok Native Americans. Within each of the major bands were numerous, smaller tribal groups, each consisting of anywhere from 60 to a few hundred people.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
5,Miwok Stories and Songs — Ronnie's Awesome ListCoast Miwok Oral TraditionETHNOHISTORY AND ETHNOGEOGRAPHY OF THE COAST MIWOK AND ... - NPSOur History - coastmiwokofmarin.org,https://www.ronniesawesomelist.com/ronnies-awesome-list/miwok-stories-and-songs,"Sep 25, 2021 · Join us for a fun, family-friendly program as we listen to traditional Coast Miwok folktales and sing songs in the Coast Miwok language. This program will be led by Alicia Mary Retes, storytelling performer throughout the Greater Bay Area, and a Mayo/Yaqui descendant. In addition to contributing to a basic knowledge of the oral traditions of the Coast Miwok, the article aims to situate these traditions in the broader context of Central California practices, while also commenting on their general ethnopoetic features. The original scope of work for this study called for the analysis and synthesis of sources identifying the Coast Miwok tribal communities that inhabited GGNRA parklands in Marin County prior to Spanish colonization. Loosely divided into five major bands inhabiting the general area that includes today’s Marin and southern Sonoma counties, Coast Miwok were the second largest group of Miwok Native Americans. What is a Coast Miwok? Coast Miwok included the Bodega Bay Miwok, or Olamentko (Olamentke), from authenticated Miwok villages around Bodega Bay, the Marin Miwok, or Hookooeko (Huukuiko), and Southern Sonoma Miwok, or Lekahtewutko (Lekatuit). Where were Coast Miwok communities located? This report documents the locations of Spanish-contact period Coast Miwok regional and local communities in lands of present Marin and Sonoma counties, California. When was Coast Miwok written? It is written almost 100 years after Samuel Barrett published the first details on Coast Miwok ethnogeography (1908a) and more than 70 years after Isabel Kelly gathered a rich body of notes about Coast Miwok village locations (in Collier and Thalman 1991). The current study is not merely a new listing of village locations. Where can I find detailed information about Coast Miwok ethnogeography? His detailed information regarding Coast Miwok ethnogeography is found in “The Ethnogeography of the Pomo and Neighboring Indians” (Barrett 1908a:305-314). In that work, he discussed many ancient and historic village locations without attributing specific pieces of information to individually named Indian consultants.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
6,ETHNOHISTORY AND ETHNOGEOGRAPHY OF THE COAST MIWOK AND ... - NPS,https://home.nps.gov/goga/learn/historyculture/upload/2009-Final-Coast-Miwok-Report.pdf,The original scope of work for this study called for the analysis and synthesis of sources identifying the Coast Miwok tribal communities that inhabited GGNRA parklands in Marin County prior to Spanish colonization.,"Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
7,- The Coast Miwok — San Geronimo Valley Historical Society,https://www.sgvhistoricalsociety.org/coast-miwok,"Coast Miwok descendent Sky Road Webb, who traces his ancestry to the Miwok of the Tomales Bay region, performs music in the tradition of his people, both ancient songs and his newly composed songs - all in the Coast Miwok language.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
8,The Daily Life and Traditions of the Coast Miwok: Exploring Cultural Richness – Marin County Visitor,https://www.marincountyvisitor.com/the-daily-life-and-traditions-of-the-coast-miwok/,"May 23, 2025 - Music was integral to cultural expression, often featuring traditional instruments and songs performed during important gatherings and ceremonies. These performances were not only entertainment but also a means of preserving their cultural heritage through sound and rhythm. The Coast Miwok, indigenous to Marin County, relied on the natural resources around them for survival.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
9,"Coast Miwok • Mill Valley, CA",https://www.millvalleylibrary.org/CivicAlerts.asp?AID=1243,"January 23, 2025 - The Spanish named them “Miwok” after hearing the Natives call themselves “Michako,” meaning “we are the people.” The Coast Miwok comprised three groups: the Hookooekoo of Marin County, the Olamentko of Bodega Bay, and the Lekahtewutko near Petaluma.","Miwok, Coast",Miwok-Costanoan
